tel 1.0

base anticipation
import soundness

info   the interface typeclasses libraries agree on, so that each can accept the others' types without depending on them
detail
    _Anticipation_ holds the small typeclasses through which Soundness's libraries, and
    libraries outside it, exchange values without depending on one another. A library which
    opens a file needs *some* path, a library which sleeps needs *some* duration, and a
    library which fetches needs *some* URL; none of them should have to choose between
    galilei's `Path`, `java.nio.file.Path` and `java.io.File`, or between aviation's
    `Instant`, `java.time.Instant` and a `Long`. Each asks instead for a type which is
    `Abstractable across Paths to Text`, or `Instantiable across Instants from Long`, and the
    library that owns the concrete type provides the instance.

    The typeclasses live here, apart from every implementation, because the dependency must
    point *into* a common, almost empty module from both sides. If `galilei` defined the path
    typeclass, a library accepting paths would depend on the whole filesystem library; if the
    library defined it, `galilei` would depend on that. Here neither depends on the other:
    both depend on `anticipation`, which depends on almost nothing.

    Two typeclasses carry most of the traffic, each parameterised by a **domain**, a sealed
    marker type which names the concept being exchanged (`Paths`, `Urls`, `Instants`,
    `Durations`, `Uuids`, `Hostnames`, `EmailAddresses`, `IpAddresses`, `HttpStreams`, …):

    - `Abstractable across Domain to Result`: a value becomes its **generic** representation,
      `value.generic`: text for a path or URL, nanoseconds for a duration, epoch milliseconds
      for an instant.
    - `Instantiable across Domain from Origin`: a generic representation becomes a value of
      the caller's chosen type, `target(origin)` inside a method with the bound
      `[target: Instantiable across Paths from Text]`.

    So a value of one library's type is converted to another's by abstracting it and
    instantiating the result, with neither library knowing of the other. A galilei path's
    generic form is its text, `t"/home/work"`, and that text becomes a `java.nio.file.Path` or
    a `java.io.File` according to the path interface imported:

    ```scala fuel
    alternative `java.nio.file.Path`
      code
          import soundness.*
          import strategies.throwUnsafely
          import pathInterfaces.javaNioPath

          def relocate[target: Instantiable across Paths from Text](path: Path on Linux)
          :   target =
            target(path.generic)

          val path: Path on Linux = t"/home/work".as[Path on Linux]
          val nio: java.nio.file.Path = relocate[java.nio.file.Path](path)
    alternative `java.io.File`
      code
          import soundness.*
          import strategies.throwUnsafely
          import pathInterfaces.javaIoFile

          def relocate[target: Instantiable across Paths from Text](path: Path on Linux)
          :   target =
            target(path.generic)

          val path: Path on Linux = t"/home/work".as[Path on Linux]
          val file: java.io.File = relocate[java.io.File](path)
    ##
    ```

    A Soundness type's own instances live in its companion (`Path.generic`, `Url.abstractable`,
    `Instant.generic`), so they need no import. But a method bounded `[target: Instantiable
    across Paths from Text]` names no concrete type whose companion could be searched, and a
    Java type has no companion Soundness can write to, so the instances which *choose* a
    representation are offered in **choice packages**, one per domain, and imported by name:
    `pathInterfaces`, `urlInterfaces`, `instantInterfaces`, `durationInterfaces`,
    `uuidInterfaces`, `hostnameInterfaces`, `emailAddressInterfaces` and
    `ipAddressInterfaces`. Each package is declared in `anticipation` by the library which
    owns the type it offers: diuretic contributes the Java types (`javaNioPath`,
    `javaTimeInstant`, …), aviation its `Instant` and `Quantity`, galilei and serpentine the
    path representatives, inimitable `Uuid`, and urticose its URL and network types. Their
    names follow the house provenance words: a Java type keeps its type-path name
    (`javaNioPath`, `javaUtilDate`), and Soundness's own type is `soundness…`
    (`soundnessUrl`, `soundnessUuid`). The givens are imported by name, because `import
    pkg.*` does not import givens: `import pathInterfaces.javaNioPath`.

    A bare `Long` cannot say whether it counts nanoseconds or milliseconds, so it has no
    unit-agnostic instance: `abstractables` and `instantiables` hold one given per unit,
    named for it, and a file imports exactly one duration reading.

    The rest of the module is the same idea applied to other concepts: `Text` itself (the
    opaque string every library shares), `Data` for bytes, `Encodable` for encoding into a
    named format, `Loggable`/`LogSink`/`Transcribable` for logging without a logging
    runtime, `Printable` and `Termcap` for terminal rendering, `Chromatic`, `Chroma` and the
    pixel `Channel`s for colour, `Graphical` for images, `Focusable` for browser locators,
    `Nominable` for names, `Textualizable` for text conversion, and `Durable` for
    capabilities safe to share across threads.

    The library is split into many small, platform-neutral components, so that a library
    depends only on the concepts it exchanges: `anticipation.generic` (`Abstractable`,
    `Instantiable`, `Representative`), `text`, `opaque`, `codec`, `color`, `gfx`, `focus`,
    `http`, `log`, `net`, `path`, `print`, `time`, `url` and `uuid`. Everything is exported
    to the `soundness` umbrella except `Channel`, which clashes with another library's and is
    reached as `anticipation.Channel`; the choice packages merge with the umbrella's own
    blocks of the same names.

refer anticipation.Abstractable
refer anticipation.Instantiable
refer anticipation.Representative
refer anticipation.Text
refer anticipation.pathInterfaces
refer anticipation.instantInterfaces

entry .Abstractable   a typeclass turning a value into the generic representation of a domain
  detail
      `value is Abstractable across Domain to Result` says that `value` can be reduced to a
      `Result`, the representation every library agrees on for `Domain`: `Text` for `Paths`
      and `Urls`, a `Long` of nanoseconds for `Durations`, a `Long` of milliseconds since the
      epoch for `Instants`, an `HttpStreams.Content` for `HttpStreams`. A library which
      *consumes* paths, durations or URLs bounds its parameter by it and calls `#generic`,
      accepting any type with an instance and depending on none of them.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      def describe[path: Abstractable across Paths to Text](path: path): Text =
        t"the path ${path.generic}"
      ```

      Instances live in the companion of the abstracted type (`Path.generic`,
      `Url.abstractable`, `Moment.generic`), so they are found without an import; a Java
      type's instance is offered in a choice package such as `pathInterfaces` or
      `instantInterfaces`. A bare `Long` duration or instant has instances only in
      `abstractables`, one per unit, imported by name.

      `Abstractable` is a pure typeclass: an instance captures nothing.
  refer anticipation.Instantiable
  refer anticipation.abstractables
  entry #genericize   reduces a value to its generic representation
      The method an instance implements; `#generic` is the extension a caller writes.
  entry #generic   the generic representation of a value: `value.generic`
      An extension on the abstracted type. `path.generic` is the path as text,
      `duration.generic` its nanoseconds, `instant.generic` its epoch milliseconds.
  entry #contramap   an instance for another type, by first mapping it to this one
      `abstractable.contramap[Wrapper](_.inner)` abstracts a `Wrapper` across the same domain
      to the same result. The function must be pure.

entry .Instantiable   a typeclass building a value from the generic representation of a domain
  detail
      `target is Instantiable across Domain from Origin` says that a `target` can be made from
      an `Origin`, the representation every library agrees on for `Domain`. A library which
      *produces* paths, instants or URLs bounds its result type by it, so the caller chooses
      the type it receives:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      def home[path: Instantiable across Paths from Text]: path = path(t"/home/work")
      ```

      The context-bound name is itself the instance, so `path(text)` applies it. Instances
      for a Soundness type are in its companion; but where a method's bound names no concrete
      type, the choice of representation is made by importing an instance by name from a
      choice package, as `import pathInterfaces.javaNioPath` or `import
      urlInterfaces.soundnessUrl` does. An instance which parses (a `Uuid`, a `Hostname`, an
      `HttpUrl`) needs a `Tactic` for its error and captures it, which is why
      `Instantiable` is a tracked typeclass rather than a pure one.
  refer anticipation.Abstractable
  refer anticipation.instantiables
  refer anticipation.Representative
  entry #apply   builds a value from its generic representation
  entry #instantiate   builds a value from its generic representation: `origin.instantiate`
      An extension on the origin type, available where exactly one applicable instance is in
      scope; with several, apply the instance (`#apply`) instead.
  entry #map   an instance for another type, by mapping each value this one builds
      The result captures this instance and the function.

entry .Representative   evidence that a type may stand for a domain, such as a path type for `Paths`
  detail
      `path is Representative of Paths` marks a type as an acceptable representation of a
      domain where the API must name the type the caller wants without converting anything
      yet: `workingDirectory[path: Representative of Paths]` and
      `temporaryDirectory[path: Representative of Paths]` in ambience accept only a type
      with this evidence, and then instantiate it through `Paths.Resolver`. It is a sealed,
      memberless marker, whose instances are erased (`inline given … = !!`) and cost nothing.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import systems.javaBaseSystem
      import workingDirectories.systemWorkingDirectory

      val directory = workingDirectory[Path on Linux]
      ```

      galilei's platform paths (`Path on Linux`, `Path on Windows`, `Path on MacOs`, `Path on
      Local`, `Path on Posix`) carry their evidence in their platform's companion and in
      `pathInterfaces`; serpentine makes `Text` a representative (`pathInterfaces.textPath`),
      and diuretic `java.nio.file.Path` and `java.io.File`.
  refer anticipation.Paths
  refer anticipation.pathInterfaces

entry .Paths   the domain of filesystem paths, whose generic representation is text
  detail
      A sealed marker naming the concept of a path, used as the `Domain` of `Abstractable`,
      `Instantiable` and `Representative`. A path's generic form is its text, as the
      filesystem writes it: `path is Abstractable across Paths to Text` reads a path of any
      type, and `path is Instantiable across Paths from Text` makes one. A second origin,
      `Paths.Trusted`, marks text which came from the operating system and so needs no
      validation.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      def open[path: Abstractable across Paths to Text](path: path): Text = path.generic
      ```

      Instances: galilei and serpentine's `Path` in its companion; `java.nio.file.Path` and
      `java.io.File` in `pathInterfaces`, from diuretic.
  refer anticipation.pathInterfaces
  refer anticipation.Representative
  entry .Trusted   path text known to be valid, such as a directory the operating system reported
    detail
        An `Instantiable` origin distinct from plain `Text`: a path type which is
        `Instantiable across Paths from Paths.Trusted` can be made from it without the
        validation (and the `Tactic`) which instantiating from untrusted text needs.
        serpentine's `Path` has such an instance; `Paths.Resolver` prefers it.
    entry #text   the trusted path text
  entry .Resolver   resolves a directory's text into the requested path type, preferring trusted instantiation
    detail
        The contextual value behind ambience's `workingDirectory` and `temporaryDirectory`:
        given the text of a directory the operating system reported, `resolver(text)`
        builds the caller's chosen path type. Its companion's `.trusted` instance, through
        `Instantiable across Paths from Trusted`, outranks the fallback `fromText` instance
        inherited from `Resolver2`, which instantiates from plain `Text`. The choice is made
        by ordinary implicit search rather than an inline `summonFrom`, so that it still
        works when the calling code is expanded inside a staged quote.
    entry .trusted   resolves through the path type's `Instantiable across Paths from Trusted` instance
    entry #apply   the path for a directory's text
  entry .Resolver2   the lower-priority parent of the `Paths.Resolver` companion
    hidden
    detail
        Holds the fallback resolver, so that the trusted one in `Paths.Resolver`'s companion
        is preferred where both apply.
    entry #fromText   resolves through the path type's `Instantiable across Paths from Text` instance

entry .Nominable   a typeclass giving a value's name as text, such as a path's final segment
  detail
      `value is Nominable` provides `#name`: for a serpentine `Path`, the last segment of its
      descent, or its root if it has none. A library which displays or sorts by name asks for
      a `Nominable` rather than for a particular path type. It is a pure typeclass.
  entry #name   the value's name

entry .Urls   the domain of URLs, whose generic representation is text
  detail
      A sealed marker naming the concept of a URL. `url is Abstractable across Urls to Text`
      reads a URL of any type as text, and `url is Instantiable across Urls from Text` makes
      one. urticose's `HttpUrl` has both in its companion, and the instantiation is also
      offered by name as `urlInterfaces.soundnessUrl`; `java.net.URL` is
      `urlInterfaces.javaNetUrl`, from diuretic.
  refer anticipation.urlInterfaces

entry .Instants   the domain of points in time, whose generic representation is epoch milliseconds
  detail
      A sealed marker naming the concept of an instant. The generic form is a `Long` of
      **milliseconds** since the Unix epoch: `instant is Abstractable across Instants to Long`
      and `instant is Instantiable across Instants from Long`. A library which schedules or
      stamps asks for one of these rather than for aviation's `Instant` or `java.time`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import instantInterfaces.javaTimeInstant

      def at[instant: Instantiable across Instants from Long](epochMilliseconds: Long): instant =
        instant(epochMilliseconds)

      at[java.time.Instant](0L)
      ```

      Instances: aviation's `Instant` and `Moment` in their companions, and by name in
      `instantInterfaces`; `java.time.Instant`, `java.util.Date` and a raw `Long` in
      `instantInterfaces`, from diuretic; a bare `Long` in `abstractables` and
      `instantiables` as `epochMillisecondsAbstractable` and `epochMillisecondsInstantiable`.
      Note the asymmetry with `Durations`, whose `Long` counts nanoseconds.
  refer anticipation.instantInterfaces
  refer anticipation.Durations

entry .Durations   the domain of lengths of time, whose generic representation is nanoseconds
  detail
      A sealed marker naming the concept of a duration. The generic form is a `Long` of
      **nanoseconds**: `duration is Abstractable across Durations to Long` and `duration is
      Instantiable across Durations from Long`. parasite's `sleep` and `snooze`, and every
      timeout in Soundness, accept any type with an instance, so an aviation `Timespan`, a
      quantitative `Quantity[Seconds[1]]` and a bare `Long` in a chosen unit all serve.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import abstractables.millisecondsAbstractable

      def nanoseconds[duration: Abstractable across Durations to Long](duration: duration): Long =
        duration.generic

      nanoseconds(200L)   // 200000000L
      ```

      A bare `Long` has no unit, so it has an instance only by import, one per unit, and the
      three share a type, so exactly one may be imported into a file. Instances: aviation's
      `Timespan` and `Quantity` in their companions, and by name as
      `durationInterfaces.aviationDuration`; a raw nanosecond `Long` as
      `durationInterfaces.javaLongDuration`, from diuretic.
  refer anticipation.abstractables
  refer anticipation.durationInterfaces
  entry .ms   converts between nanoseconds and milliseconds, inline
      Two overloads: `ms(long: Long): Double` scales a `Long` up by a million, and `ms(double:
      Double): Long` scales a `Double` down by a million, truncating. Note that the direction
      is set by the argument's type, not by the name.
  entry .ns   converts between a `Long` and a `Double` count of nanoseconds, inline
      `ns(long: Long): Double` widens; `ns(double: Double): Long` truncates.

entry .Dates   the domain of calendar dates, as a marker for `Abstractable` and `Instantiable`
    A sealed marker naming the concept of a date, with no fixed generic representation of its
    own: an instance states its `Result` or `Origin` type.

entry .Times   the domain of times of day, as a marker for `Abstractable` and `Instantiable`
    A sealed marker naming the concept of a time of day, with no fixed generic
    representation of its own: an instance states its `Result` or `Origin` type.

entry .Uuids   the domain of UUIDs, whose generic representation is text
    A sealed marker naming the concept of a UUID. `uuid is Instantiable across Uuids from
    Text` parses one; inimitable's `Uuid` is offered by name as
    `uuidInterfaces.soundnessUuid`, so a library can accept UUIDs without depending on
    inimitable.

entry .EmailAddresses   the domain of email addresses, whose generic representation is text
    A sealed marker naming the concept of an email address. urticose's `EmailAddress` is
    `Instantiable across EmailAddresses from Text` by the given
    `emailAddressInterfaces.soundnessEmailAddress`, imported by name.

entry .Hostnames   the domain of hostnames, whose generic representation is text
    A sealed marker naming the concept of a hostname. urticose's `Hostname` is `Instantiable
    across Hostnames from Text` by the given `hostnameInterfaces.soundnessHostname`,
    imported by name.

entry .IpAddresses   the domain of IP addresses, whose generic representation is text
    A sealed marker naming the concept of an IP address. urticose's `Ipv4` and `Ipv6` are
    `Instantiable across IpAddresses from Text` by the givens
    `ipAddressInterfaces.soundnessIpv4` and `ipAddressInterfaces.soundnessIpv6`, imported by
    name.

entry .HttpRequests   the domain of HTTP request bodies, as a marker for `Instantiable`
    A sealed marker naming what an HTTP server receives. telekinesis can decode a request
    body as any `content` which is `Instantiable across HttpRequests from Text`, and codec
    libraries (jacinta, xylophone, ypsiloid) provide such instances for their own types, so
    neither side depends on the other.

entry .HttpStreams   the domain of HTTP message bodies, with a minimal pull protocol for their bytes
  detail
      A sealed marker naming what is sent as an HTTP body. A type which is `Abstractable
      across HttpStreams to HttpStreams.Content` can be posted by telekinesis's client or
      served by its server: a `Content` is a media type paired with a `Body`. jacinta's
      `Json`, xylophone's `Xml` and the binary codecs provide instances in their own
      companions.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val content: HttpStreams.Content =
        (t"text/plain", HttpStreams.Body(Data(72, 105)))
      ```

      The `Body` protocol is deliberately dependency-free, so that libraries below
      turbulence can produce one; turbulence's streams adapt to and from it.
  refer anticipation.HttpRequests
  entry .Body   a demand-driven source of body bytes, pulled one bounded chunk at a time
    detail
        A `Body` is asked for its bytes with `#next(limit)`, which returns at most `limit`
        bytes as one chunk, or `null` at the end of the stream, and may block until input
        is available.
    entry .empty   the body with no bytes
    entry .apply   a body from a whole `Data` value, an iterator of chunks or a `Chain` of chunks
        From a `Data` value, the bytes are delivered in slices of at most `limit` bytes. From
        a stdlib iterator or a lazy `Chain` of chunks, each call delivers the next chunk
        whole, so `limit` does not bound its size; a body from an iterator captures it.
    entry #next   the next chunk of at most `limit` bytes, or `null` at the end of the body
  entry .Content   a body with its media type: `(Text, HttpStreams.Body)`

entry .abstractables   the readings of a bare `Long` as a duration or an instant, each a given to import by name
  detail
      A `Long` cannot say whether it counts nanoseconds, microseconds, milliseconds or
      milliseconds since the epoch, so `import soundness.*` gives it no reading at all, and a
      method which takes any `Abstractable across Durations` or `across Instants` rejects a
      bare `Long` until a unit is chosen by importing one of these by name:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import abstractables.millisecondsAbstractable

      supervise:
        snooze(200L)   // 200 milliseconds
      ```

      The three duration givens share a type, so exactly one may be imported into a file;
      importing a duration reading and `epochMillisecondsAbstractable` together makes a
      `Long` passed where either is accepted ambiguous. `instantiables` mirrors all four in
      the opposite direction.
  refer anticipation.instantiables
  refer anticipation.Durations
  entry .nanosecondsAbstractable   reads a `Long` duration as nanoseconds, the generic representation itself
  entry .microsecondsAbstractable   reads a `Long` duration as microseconds, multiplying by a thousand
  entry .millisecondsAbstractable   reads a `Long` duration as milliseconds, multiplying by a million
  entry .epochMillisecondsAbstractable   reads a `Long` instant as milliseconds since the epoch, the generic representation

entry .instantiables   the ways to receive a duration or an instant as a bare `Long`, each a given to import by name
  detail
      The `Instantiable` counterparts of `abstractables`: with one imported, a method which
      returns any `Instantiable across Durations from Long` (or `across Instants`) can return
      a `Long` in the chosen unit. Conversions to a coarser unit truncate.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import instantiables.millisecondsInstantiable

      def length[duration: Instantiable across Durations from Long]: duration =
        duration(5_000_000L)

      length[Long]   // 5L
      ```

      As with `abstractables`, the three duration givens share a type and only one may be in
      scope.
  refer anticipation.abstractables
  entry .nanosecondsInstantiable   gives a duration as a `Long` of nanoseconds, unchanged
  entry .microsecondsInstantiable   gives a duration as a `Long` of microseconds, dividing by a thousand
  entry .millisecondsInstantiable   gives a duration as a `Long` of milliseconds, dividing by a million
  entry .epochMillisecondsInstantiable   gives an instant as a `Long` of milliseconds since the epoch, unchanged

entry .pathInterfaces   the path types a path-producing API may return, each a given to import by name
  detail
      A method which returns `[path: Instantiable across Paths from Text]`, or which asks for
      `[path: Representative of Paths]`, lets its caller choose a path type; for a type with
      no companion to search, the choice is made by importing one of these givens by name,
      since `import pathInterfaces.*` does not import givens.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import pathInterfaces.javaNioPath

      def home[path: Instantiable across Paths from Text]: path = path(t"/home/work")
      val path: java.nio.file.Path = home[java.nio.file.Path]
      ```

      The package is assembled from three libraries: diuretic declares `javaNioPath` and
      `javaIoFile`, galilei the platform path representatives, and serpentine `textPath`.
  refer anticipation.Paths
  refer anticipation.Representative
  entry .javaNioPath   `java.nio.file.Path` as a path, abstracted to and instantiated from its text (diuretic)
      Both `Abstractable across Paths to Text` and `Instantiable across Paths from Text`, as
      the object `diuretic.JavaNioPath`. Abstraction gives the path's absolute form; a
      relative `java.nio.file.Path` is resolved against the JVM's working directory. JVM only.
  entry .javaIoFile   `java.io.File` as a path, abstracted to and instantiated from its text (diuretic)
      Both `Abstractable across Paths to Text` and `Instantiable across Paths from Text`, as
      the object `diuretic.JavaIoFile`; abstraction gives the file's absolute path.
  entry .pathOnLinux   `Path on Linux` as a `Representative of Paths` (galilei)
      Erased evidence; the same given is in the companion of `Linux`, where it is found
      without an import.
  entry .pathOnWindows   `Path on Windows` as a `Representative of Paths` (galilei)
      Erased evidence; the same given is in the companion of `Windows`.
  entry .pathOnMacOs   `Path on MacOs` as a `Representative of Paths` (galilei)
      Erased evidence; the same given is in the companion of `MacOs`.
  entry .pathOnLocal   `Path on Local`, the host platform's path, as a `Representative of Paths` (galilei)
      Erased evidence; the same given is in the companion of `Local`.
  entry .pathOnPosix   `Path on Posix` as a `Representative of Paths` (galilei)
      Erased evidence, for a path on any POSIX filesystem.
  entry .textPath   `Text` as a `Representative of Paths`, so a directory may be asked for as plain text (serpentine)
      With it imported, `workingDirectory[Text]` gives the working directory's text without
      choosing a path type.

entry .urlInterfaces   the URL types a URL-producing API may return, each a given to import by name
  detail
      A method bounded `[url: Instantiable across Urls from Text]` names no concrete URL type,
      so the instance is chosen by importing one of these by name.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import urlInterfaces.soundnessUrl

      def site[url: Instantiable across Urls from Text]: url = url(t"https://example.com/")
      val url: HttpUrl = site[HttpUrl]
      ```
  refer anticipation.Urls
  entry .javaNetUrl   `java.net.URL` as a URL, abstracted to and instantiated from its text (diuretic)
      Both `Abstractable across Urls to Text` and `Instantiable across Urls from Text`, as the
      object `diuretic.JavaNetUrl`; instantiation goes through `java.net.URI`, so malformed
      text throws as the Java API does.
  entry .soundnessUrl   urticose's `HttpUrl`, instantiated from text by parsing (urticose)
      `HttpUrl is Instantiable across Urls from Text`, the instance `Url.instantiable` from
      `HttpUrl`'s companion offered by name. It needs a `Tactic[Url.Error]`, raised for text
      which is not a valid URL, and captures it.

entry .instantInterfaces   the instant types a time-producing API may return, each a given to import by name
  detail
      A method bounded `[instant: Instantiable across Instants from Long]` lets the caller
      choose the instant type it receives, from a `Long` of milliseconds since the epoch.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import instantInterfaces.javaTimeInstant

      def epoch[instant: Instantiable across Instants from Long]: instant = instant(0L)
      val start: java.time.Instant = epoch[java.time.Instant]
      ```

      diuretic declares the Java types, aviation its own `Instant`.
  refer anticipation.Instants
  entry .aviationInstant   aviation's `Instant`, as epoch milliseconds in both directions (aviation)
      `(Instant over transport) is Abstractable & Instantiable across Instants to Long from
      Long`, for any `transport`, from `Instant.generic`.
  entry .javaTimeInstant   `java.time.Instant`, as epoch milliseconds in both directions (diuretic)
      Abstraction is `toEpochMilli`, so sub-millisecond precision is dropped.
  entry .javaLongInstant   a raw `Long` of epoch milliseconds, unchanged in both directions (diuretic)
      The identity: the `Long` is already the generic representation.
  entry .javaUtilDate   `java.util.Date`, as epoch milliseconds in both directions (diuretic)

entry .durationInterfaces   the duration types a time-measuring API may return, each a given to import by name
  detail
      A method bounded `[duration: Instantiable across Durations from Long]` lets the caller
      choose the duration type it receives, from a `Long` of nanoseconds.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import durationInterfaces.aviationDuration

      def second[duration: Instantiable across Durations from Long]: duration =
        duration(1_000_000_000L)

      second[Quantity[Seconds[1]]]
      ```
  refer anticipation.Durations
  refer anticipation.abstractables
  entry .aviationDuration   a quantitative `Quantity` of time, as nanoseconds in both directions (aviation)
      `Quantity[units] is Abstractable & Instantiable across Durations to Long from Long`, for
      any units normalizable to seconds: abstraction normalizes to seconds and scales to
      nanoseconds, truncating; instantiation scales back into `units`.
  entry .javaLongDuration   a raw `Long` of nanoseconds, unchanged in both directions (diuretic)
      The `Long` counts **nanoseconds**, the generic representation, unlike `javaLongInstant`,
      whose `Long` counts epoch milliseconds. For a `Long` in another unit, import a reading
      from `abstractables` or `instantiables` instead.

entry .uuidInterfaces   the UUID types a UUID-producing API may return, each a given to import by name
  detail
      A method bounded `[uuid: Instantiable across Uuids from Text]` names no concrete type,
      so its instance is imported by name.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import uuidInterfaces.soundnessUuid

      def parse[uuid: Instantiable across Uuids from Text](text: Text): uuid = uuid(text)
      parse[Uuid](t"e3b0c442-98fc-1c14-9afb-f4c8996fb924")
      ```
  refer anticipation.Uuids
  entry .soundnessUuid   inimitable's `Uuid`, parsed from text (inimitable)
      Needs a `Tactic[Uuid.Error]`, raised for text which is not a UUID, and captures it.

entry .emailAddressInterfaces   the email-address types an API may return, each a given to import by name
  detail
      For a method bounded `[email: Instantiable across EmailAddresses from Text]`, which
      names no concrete type whose companion could hold the instance.
  refer anticipation.EmailAddresses
  entry .soundnessEmailAddress   urticose's `EmailAddress`, parsed from text (urticose)
      Needs a `Tactic[EmailAddress.Error]`, raised for text which is not a valid address, and
      captures it.

entry .hostnameInterfaces   the hostname types an API may return, each a given to import by name
  detail
      For a method bounded `[hostname: Instantiable across Hostnames from Text]`, which names
      no concrete type whose companion could hold the instance.
  refer anticipation.Hostnames
  entry .soundnessHostname   urticose's `Hostname`, parsed from text (urticose)
      Needs a `Tactic[Hostname.Error]`, raised for text which is not a valid hostname, and
      captures it.

entry .ipAddressInterfaces   the IP-address types an API may return, each a given to import by name
  detail
      For a method bounded `[ip: Instantiable across IpAddresses from Text]`, which names no
      concrete type whose companion could hold the instance. The two givens instantiate
      different types, so both may be imported.
  refer anticipation.IpAddresses
  entry .soundnessIpv4   urticose's `Ipv4`, parsed from dotted-decimal text (urticose)
      Needs a `Tactic[IpAddress.Error]`, raised for text which is not an IPv4 address.
  entry .soundnessIpv6   urticose's `Ipv6`, parsed from text (urticose)
      Needs a `Tactic[IpAddress.Error]`, raised for text which is not an IPv6 address.

entry .Text   the pure, opaque string type every Soundness library exchanges text as
    `Text` is the opaque type `anticipation.internal.Text`, exported here: a `String` at
    runtime, with no `String` methods at the type level, and pure, so the capture checker
    treats a value of it as capturing nothing. Every Soundness API takes and returns `Text`
    rather than `String`, and its operations come from gossamer and the collection
    typeclasses rather than from `java.lang.String`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val greeting: Text = t"Hello"
    val legacy: Text = "world".tt
    val joined: Text = greeting+t", "+legacy
    joined.s   // back to a String, for a Java API
    ```

    A `String` converts implicitly where a `Text` is expected (`Text.conversion`), and `tt`
    and `s` convert explicitly. Its companion supplies `+` with `Text` and with `String`,
    `*` by an `Int`, the empty text as `Zeroic`, the collection typeclasses (`Traversable by
    Char`, `Reshapable`, `Inclusive`, `Reversible`), equality with itself and with
    `String`, a `Typeable` so that `case text: Text` matches a `String`, and `ToExpr` and
    `FromExpr` for macros. All are in implicit scope; none needs an import.

    A macro author should know that `Expr(text)` lifts through a quote typed inside
    `Text`'s own scope, where the alias is transparent, so the spliced tree is typed as
    `String & caps.Pure`; at an expansion site compiled with separation checking that fails
    as `Text`. Lift the `String` and convert instead: `'{${Expr(text.s)}.tt}`.

entry .tt   converts a `String` to `Text`: `"…".tt`
    The explicit conversion, a runtime no-op. Prefer a `t"…"` literal for constant text.

entry .ss   converts a collection of `Text` to a collection of `String`, for Java and stdlib APIs
    `texts.ss` maps every element with `Text.s`, as an `Iterable[String]`.

entry .subsumes   whether a `Text` contains another as a substring
    `text.subsumes(t"lo")` is substring containment through `String.indexOf`, with no
    traversal and no interim collections, which is why it is a separate extension from the
    generic collection `subsumes` (still importable as `murmuration.subsumes`).

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    t"Hello".subsumes(t"ell")   // true
    ```

entry .Textualizable   a typeclass converting a value to `Text`, for APIs which take anything textual
  detail
      `value is Textualizable` provides the extension `#textual`, so an API can accept any
      value with a canonical textual form without depending on the library that defines it.
      Unlike `Showable`, which renders for a human, a `Textualizable` value *is* text in
      another guise.
  entry #convert   the value as `Text`
  entry #textual   the value as `Text`: `value.textual`
  entry #contramap   an instance for another type, by first mapping it to this one

entry .Durable   the classifier of shared capabilities which may be retained across a thread boundary
    A capture-checking classifier (a `caps.SharedCapability`) for monitors, running
    processes, connections, channels, loggers and log sinks. A task's body runs on another
    thread and is retained by a worker, so it is typed `->{caps.any.only[Durable]}`: it may
    capture `Durable` capabilities but not, for example, a `Tactic`, which is a
    `caps.Control` capability holding a return point on its installer's stack. A task
    raises only through its own tactic.

    A library declares its own long-lived capability by extending `Durable`; one meant to
    be ambient is provided by a given that is a *value* (or an object), not a polymorphic
    method, since a value is a static capability visible from every level.

entry .Bidi   the reading direction of a positional operation: from the start or from the end
    An enum with the cases `Ltr`, which anchors at the start, and `Rtl`, which anchors at
    the end. It is the vocabulary of the generic positional operations (`keep`, `skip`,
    `snip`) on collections and on text, so that both share one type.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    t"filename.txt".keep(3, Bidi.Rtl)   // t"txt"
    ```

    `Ltr` is the default wherever a direction is optional. The umbrella exports `Bidi`
    through gossamer.

entry .internal   the home of the opaque `Text` type and its companion instances
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: `Text` is exported from here to the `anticipation` package, and its
      page there has the full account.
  entry .Text   the opaque string type; see `anticipation.Text`
    detail
        The opaque type `Text <: Matchable & caps.Pure`, a `String` at runtime. Its
        companion holds the instances which make it a first-class text type.
    entry .apply   makes `Text` from a `String`, an array of characters or an array of ASCII bytes
        Bytes are decoded as US-ASCII; for another charset use a decoder.
    entry .s   the `Text` as a `String`: `text.s`
    entry .zeroic   the empty text as `Text`'s zero
    entry .concatenable   `text+text`, concatenating two `Text`s
    entry .addableString   `text+string`, appending a `String` to a `Text`
    entry .multiplicable   `text*n`, repeating a `Text` `n` times; a negative count gives the empty text
    entry .fromString   lets a `@main` method take `Text` parameters from the command line
    entry .traversable   traverses a `Text` as its `Char`s
    entry .reshapable   rebuilds a `Text` from a traversal of `Char`s
    entry .inclusive   whether a `Text` contains a `Char`
    entry .convertible   collects any traversal of `Char`s into a `Text`
    entry .reversible   reverses a `Text`, keeping surrogate pairs intact
    entry .fromExpr   reads a constant `Text` from a macro's `Expr`
    entry .toExpr   lifts a `Text` into a macro's `Expr`
        The lifted tree is typed as `String & caps.Pure`; under separation checking at the
        expansion site, lift the `String` and convert with `.tt` instead.
    entry .conversion   converts a `String` to `Text` wherever a `Text` is expected
    entry .canEqual   permits `==` between two `Text`s under strict equality
    entry .canEqual2   permits `==` between a `String` and a `Text` under strict equality
    entry .canEqual3   permits `==` between a `Text` and a `String` under strict equality
    entry .typeable   lets a pattern `case text: Text` match any `String`

entry .Data   immutable bytes: an `Array[Byte]` which captures nothing
  detail
      `Data` is the type alias `Array[Byte]^{}`, the byte sequence every Soundness library
      reads, writes, hashes and encodes. It is built with `apply`, `.build` or `.fill`, and
      a block whose length is a literal is branded `Sized[n]`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val header: Data = Data(0x50, 0x4b, 0x03, 0x04)
      val zeros = Data.fill(16)(_ => 0)
      ```
  refer anticipation.Encodable
  refer anticipation.bytestream
  entry .apply   `Data` from the bytes given
  entry .build   `Data` of a given length, filled in by a function writing into a fresh array
      `Data.build(n)(array => …)` allocates an `n`-byte array, passes it to the function to
      populate, and returns it frozen. With a literal length the result is branded
      `Data & Sized[n]`; with a non-literal one it is plain `Data`.
  entry .fill   `Data` of a given length, each byte computed from its index
      With a literal length the result is branded `Data & Sized[n]`.

entry .Encodable   a typeclass encoding a value into a named format, `value.in[Format]`
  detail
      `value is Encodable in Form` says that `value` can be encoded as a `Form`: `Text`,
      `Data`, `Json`, `Xml`, a binary protocol, or any carrier type a codec library defines.
      Encoding is a value-level operation, `value.in[Json]` (`in`), and `#encode` where the
      instance is in hand. Codec libraries provide instances, often derived, in their format's
      companion.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      42.in[Text]   // t"42"
      ```

      The companion gives the primitives as `Text` (their `toString`), `Data` as itself, and
      a `Long` as its eight big-endian bytes. `Encodable` is a tracked typeclass: an instance
      derived from codecs which raise retains their `Tactic`.
  refer anticipation.in
  refer anticipation.Schematic
  entry .bytes   `Data` encodes in `Data` as itself
  entry .sizedBytes   `Data & Sized[n]` encodes in `Data` as itself
  entry .longData   a `Long` encodes in `Data` as its eight bytes, big-endian
  entry .int   an `Int` encodes in `Text` as its decimal form
  entry .double   a `Double` encodes in `Text` by `toString`
  entry .byte   a `Byte` encodes in `Text` as its decimal form
  entry .short   a `Short` encodes in `Text` as its decimal form
  entry .long   a `Long` encodes in `Text` as its decimal form
  entry .float   a `Float` encodes in `Text` by `toString`
  entry .text   `Text` encodes in `Text` as itself
  entry .char   a `Char` encodes in `Text` as a one-character text
  entry .string   a `String` encodes in `Text` as the same text
  entry #encoded   encodes a value into the format
  entry #encode   encodes a value into the format: `value.encode`
  entry #contramap   an instance for another type, by first mapping it to this one
      The result captures this instance and the function.

entry .in   encodes a value into the named format: `value.in[Json]`
    The value-level counterpart of `value is Encodable in Format`. It replaces both
    `.encode`, whose format is implicit in the instance found, and per-format shorthands such
    as `.json`, naming the target format at the call.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val text: Text = 42.in[Text]
    val bytes: Data = 42L.in[Data]
    ```

entry .bytestream   a value as bytes, through its `Encodable in Data` instance
    `value.bytestream` is `value.in[Data]`.

entry .Schematic   a typeclass giving the schema of a value's encoding, in a chosen schema representation
  detail
      `value is Schematic over JsonSchema` provides `#schema`, the schema of the value's
      encoding in the `Transport` representation (`JsonSchema` in jacinta, `Tels.Type` in
      stratiform). It is the sibling of `Encodable`, and the two are commonly required
      together, as `Encodable & Schematic in Json over JsonSchema`, so that a value's
      encoding and its schema come from the same derivation. Format-specific instances live
      with their format.
  refer anticipation.Morphology
  entry #schema   the schema, in the `Transport` representation

entry .Schema   a typeclass for a schema type which parses text into a typed form
  detail
      A schema whose `Form` has a `Topic` type member: `#parse` reads a document as text into
      a `Form of Topic`, typed by the schema.
  entry #Form   the parsed form, refined with a `Topic` type member
  entry #parse   parses a lazy chain of text into the schema's typed form

entry .Morphology   the format-neutral shape of an encoding, carried by a codec beside its schema
  detail
      A codec (jacinta's `Json.Encodable`, stratiform's TEL codecs) carries the morphology of
      the values it encodes, so that the codec and the schema derived from it are coherent by
      construction; the morphology is then reified into a format-specific schema, a
      `JsonSchema` or a `Tels.Type`, downstream. It records structure only; descriptions,
      formats and bounds belong to each format's own schema.

      The leaf cases are `Str` (a string), `Whole` (an integer), `Real` (a fractional
      number), `Bool`, `Empty` (null or unit) and `Any` (unconstrained); the compound ones
      follow.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val person = Morphology.Obj(List(t"name" -> Morphology.Str, t"age" -> Morphology.Whole),
          List(t"name", t"age"))
      ```
  entry #optional   whether a value of this shape may be absent: true only for `Opt`
      Used to compute which fields of a record are required.
  entry .Opt   an optional value of a shape
    entry #shape   the shape when present
  entry .Arr   a homogeneous sequence
    entry #items   the shape of each element
  entry .Dict   a map of arbitrary keys to values
    entry #key     the shape of the keys
    entry #value   the shape of the values
  entry .Obj   a record of named fields, with those which are required
    entry #fields     the fields' names and shapes, in order
    entry #required   the names of the fields which must be present
  entry .OneOf   a tagged disjunction of shapes
    entry #variants   the shape of each variant

entry .Underlying   erased evidence that an opaque type is represented by a primitive
    `Underlying[Alias, Primitive]` is declared, erased, in an opaque type's companion (as
    `Chroma` declares `Underlying[Chroma, Int]`) to permit `underlying`, which views a value
    of the alias as its representation at no cost. It is sealed and has no runtime value.

entry .underlying   views an opaque value as its primitive representation, where the alias permits it
    `value.underlying[Int]` compiles only where an erased `Underlying[Alias, Int]` is in
    scope, and is a cast with no runtime cost.

entry .Log   logs an event at one of four levels, and names the categories an event may belong to
  detail
      `Log.fine`, `Log.info`, `Log.warn` and `Log.fail` log a value through the `Loggable`
      instance in scope, which `Loggable.fanOut` derives in implicit scope from every
      `LogSink` present. The argument is taken by name and the level is checked first, so
      when no sink accepts the level, including when there is no sink at all, the value is
      never constructed: a disabled log statement costs nothing and needs no guard.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      def report(remote: Text): Unit =
        Log.fine(m"starting...")
        Log.info(m"connected to $remote")
      ```

      A library declares that a method logs its own event type with `logs`, as in `Long logs
      Backup.Event`.

      The object also holds a growable vocabulary of **categories** describing the nature of
      an event: an event type, or one of its enum cases, mixes in one or more of the marker
      traits (`Log.Network`, `Log.Filesystem`, …), and a sink configured with a set of
      `Log.Category` values (such as eucalyptus's `Logger(…, categories = Set(Log.Network))`)
      records only events whose runtime type mixes one in. Each trait is paired with an
      object of the same name, the `Category` value carrying its runtime class. The filter
      crosses libraries, so every library's network events can be selected at once.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      enum Fetch:
        case Requested(url: Text) extends Fetch, Log.Network
        case Cached(url: Text) extends Fetch, Log.Network, Log.Cache
      ```
  refer anticipation.Loggable
  refer anticipation.LogSink
  refer anticipation.logs
  entry .fine   logs a value at `Log.Level.Fine`: high-frequency detail, useful while debugging
  entry .info   logs a value at `Log.Level.Info`: one line per significant operation
  entry .warn   logs a value at `Log.Level.Warn`: a recoverable or unexpected-but-handled condition
  entry .fail   logs a value at `Log.Level.Fail`: an operation which failed unrecoverably
  entry .Category   a category of log event, carrying the runtime class of its marker trait
    detail
        Each category is an object paired with a marker trait of the same name; a sink
        tests an event against a category with `#reference`, at runtime, since the call site
        knows only the event's general type.
    entry #reference   the runtime class of the category's marker trait
  entry .Memory   events about memory use and allocation, as a marker trait and its `Category`
  entry .Cpu   events about processor use, as a marker trait and its `Category`
  entry .Threading   events about threads, tasks and their scheduling, as a marker trait and its `Category`
  entry .Process   events about operating-system processes, as a marker trait and its `Category`
  entry .Filesystem   events about files and directories, as a marker trait and its `Category`
  entry .Disk   events about storage devices and volumes, as a marker trait and its `Category`
  entry .Network   events about network connections and traffic, as a marker trait and its `Category`
  entry .Database   events about database access, as a marker trait and its `Category`
  entry .Cache   events about caching, hits and evictions, as a marker trait and its `Category`
  entry .Serialization   events about encoding and decoding, as a marker trait and its `Category`
  entry .Crypto   events about cryptographic operations, as a marker trait and its `Category`
  entry .Auth   events about authentication and authorization, as a marker trait and its `Category`
  entry .Configuration   events about reading configuration, as a marker trait and its `Category`
  entry .Dependency   events about resolving dependencies, as a marker trait and its `Category`
  entry .Scheduler   events about scheduled and periodic work, as a marker trait and its `Category`
  entry .Time   events about clocks and timing, as a marker trait and its `Category`
  entry .Ui   events about the user interface, as a marker trait and its `Category`
  entry .Protocol   events about protocol messages and handshakes, as a marker trait and its `Category`
  entry .Compiler   events about compilation, as a marker trait and its `Category`
  entry .Runtime   events about the runtime environment, such as loading code, as a marker trait and its `Category`
  entry .Gc   events about garbage collection, as a marker trait and its `Category`
  entry .Resource   events about reading bundled resources, as a marker trait and its `Category`
  entry .Security   events about security-relevant conditions, as a marker trait and its `Category`

  entry .Level   the severity of a log event: `Fine`, `Info`, `Warn` or `Fail`
    detail
        An enum, ordered from least to most severe. `Fine` is high-frequency detail, normally
        switched off; `Info` one line per significant operation; `Warn` a recoverable or
        unexpected-but-handled condition; `Fail` an operation which failed unrecoverably,
        logged beside the error being raised. A sink's threshold (`LogSink#accepts`) drops
        everything below a chosen level, as eucalyptus's `Logger(Out, level = Log.Level.Warn)` does.
    entry .comparable   orders levels by severity, from `Fine` to `Fail`

entry .Loggable   a typeclass logging events of one type, derived from the sinks in scope
  detail
      `event is Loggable` is what `Log.info` and its siblings need. It is rarely written by
      hand: `.fanOut`, in implicit scope, derives one by transcribing the event once to each
      sink's carrier type (`Transcribable`) and fanning it out to every `LogSink` in scope,
      so several sinks in scope each receive every event, each applying its own threshold.
      With no sink in scope the derived instance is silent. An explicitly imported instance
      outranks the derived one: `import logging.silentLogging`, from eucalyptus, suppresses
      all logging in a file.

      A `Loggable` is a shared capability (`Durable`): it is written to from every task at
      once, and captures the sinks it fans out to, which is why `logs` makes its evidence a
      capturing one.
  refer anticipation.LogSink
  refer anticipation.Transcribable
  refer anticipation.logs
  entry .silent   a logger for an event type which records nothing, as a pure value
  entry .fanOut   derives a logger from every `LogSink` in scope, through the event's `Transcribable`
      For each logged event, if any sink accepts its level, the by-name event is forced once
      and, unless `Transcribable#skip` rejects it, transcribed lazily to the carrier and
      submitted to every sink whose threshold and categories (`LogSink#admits`) accept it.
  entry #log   logs an event at a level and timestamp
  entry #contramap   a logger for another type, by first mapping each event to this one
      The function may capture only `Durable` capabilities, since the logger retains it.

entry .LogSink   a destination for log events of a carrier type, such as eucalyptus's `Logger`
  detail
      A `LogSink[Event, Carrier]` records events transcribed to `Carrier` (a `Message`,
      say). It is the abstraction `Loggable.fanOut` gathers from scope, so this module
      needs no logging runtime; eucalyptus's `Logger` implements it. It is contravariant in
      its event type, so a `LogSink[Any, Message]` collects every event.

      A sink is a `Durable` capability: it is shared and synchronised, since every task
      writes to it, and ambient, typically an imported package given.
  refer anticipation.Loggable
  entry #accepts   whether the sink records events at a level
      Consulted before the event is forced, so a rejected event is never constructed.
  entry #admits   whether the sink records this particular event; true unless overridden
      Takes the original event rather than its transcription, so that a sink filtered to
      `Log.Category`s can test the event's runtime type.
  entry #submit   records an event, already transcribed, at a level and timestamp

entry .Transcribable   a typeclass transcribing an event into a sink's carrier type
  detail
      `event is Transcribable to Carrier` converts an event into the type a `LogSink`
      records, and is how one library's events are carried inside another's. `transcribes`
      reads more naturally in a given:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      enum Inner:
        case Copied(source: Text)

      enum Outer:
        case Ran(event: Inner)

      given (Outer transcribes Inner) = Outer.Ran(_)
      ```

      A message type is transcribable from any `Communicable` event, which is why logging an
      `m"…"` message needs no declaration.
  refer anticipation.transcribes
  entry #record   transcribes an event into the carrier type
  entry #skip   whether to drop an event rather than transcribe it; false unless overridden
  entry #contramap   an instance for another type, by first mapping each event to this one

entry .logs   the type of a result computed by code that logs events of a type: `Result logs Event`
    `Long logs Backup.Event` is a context function needing a capturing `Backup.Event is
    Loggable`, so a caller must either declare the event itself or have a sink for it in
    scope. Declarations chain: `Unit logs Http.Event logs Exec.Event`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    enum Backup:
      case Started(source: Text)

    def backup(source: Text): Unit logs Backup =
      Log.info(Backup.Started(source))
    ```

entry .transcribes   the type of a `Transcribable` instance read in the direction of the wrapping
    `Outer transcribes Inner` is `Inner is Transcribable to Outer`: a given of it lets
    `Inner` events be logged where `Outer` events are accepted, wrapped as `Outer`.

entry .Printable   a typeclass rendering a value as text for a terminal of known capabilities
  detail
      `value is Printable` renders a value to `Text` given a `Termcap`, so that a value which
      may carry colour or styling (an escapade `Teletype`, a `Message`) degrades to plain
      text on a terminal without ANSI support. turbulence's `Out.println` and `Err.println`
      take any `Printable` value, with a `Termcap` in scope.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import termcapDefinitions.basicTermcap
      import stdios.javaLangSystemStdio

      Out.println(t"Hello")
      ```

      The companion makes `Text`, `String` and `Char` printable as themselves; escapade
      provides instances for its styled text.
  refer anticipation.Termcap
  entry .text   `Text` prints as itself, on any terminal
  entry .string   a `String` prints as the same text
  entry .char   a `Char` prints as a one-character text
  entry #print   renders a value for a terminal with the given capabilities
  entry #contramap   an instance for another type, by first mapping each value, with the `Termcap` in context
      The function is a context function of the `Termcap`, so the mapping itself can depend
      on what the terminal supports.

entry .Termcap   the capabilities of a terminal: ANSI support, colour depth and size
  detail
      A `Termcap` in scope tells a `Printable` how to render: whether ANSI escapes are
      understood, how many colours are available, and the terminal's width and height, which
      default to unbounded (`Int.MaxValue`), so that nothing wraps. A fixed one is imported
      by name from `termcapDefinitions`; ambience's `termcaps.environmentTermcap` reads the
      terminal's capabilities from the environment, and a daemon's invocation carries its
      client's.
  refer anticipation.termcapDefinitions
  refer anticipation.ColorDepth
  entry #ansi    whether the terminal understands ANSI escape sequences
  entry #color   how many colours the terminal can show
  entry #width   the terminal's width in columns; unbounded unless known
  entry #height   the terminal's height in rows; unbounded unless known

entry .ColorDepth   how many colours a terminal can display, from none to 24-bit true colour
  detail
      An enum, from least to most capable: `NoColor`; `Indexed8` and `Indexed16`, the basic
      ANSI palettes; `Cube4` and `Cube6`, the 88- and 256-colour palettes with a colour cube;
      and `TrueColor`, 24-bit colour.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      ColorDepth(256)   // ColorDepth.Cube6
      ```
  entry .apply   the colour depth for a terminal's colour count, as `tput colors` reports it
      8 is `Indexed8`; 15 or 16 `Indexed16`; 52, 64 or 88 `Cube4`; 256 `Cube6`; 65536 or
      16777216 `TrueColor`; anything else `NoColor`.

entry .termcapDefinitions   fixed terminal capabilities, each a given to import by name
  detail
      For a program which knows what its output is rendered on, or a test or document whose
      output must not depend on the terminal running it. Each is a given imported by name,
      since `import termcapDefinitions.*` does not import givens:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import termcapDefinitions.basicTermcap
      ```

      None reports a width or height, so text never wraps.
  refer anticipation.Termcap
  entry .basicTermcap   a terminal with no ANSI support and no colour: plain text
  entry .xterm256Termcap   an ANSI terminal with the 256-colour palette
  entry .xtermTrueColorTermcap   an ANSI terminal with 24-bit true colour

entry .Chromatic   a typeclass reducing a colour of any representation to a packed 24-bit RGB `Chroma`
  detail
      `color is Chromatic` lets an API which draws or styles accept a colour from any
      library: cataclysm's CSS, savagery's SVG and escapade's terminal styling each take a
      `Chromatic` value, and iridescence's colour spaces and pixels provide instances.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      def hex[color: Chromatic](color: color): Int = color.chroma.underlying

      hex(Chroma(255, 128, 0))
      ```
  refer anticipation.Chroma
  entry #convert   the colour as a `Chroma`
  entry #chroma   the colour as a `Chroma`: `color.chroma`
  entry #red     the colour's red component, 0 to 255
  entry #green   the colour's green component, 0 to 255
  entry #blue    the colour's blue component, 0 to 255
  entry #contramap   an instance for another type, by first mapping it to a colour of this one

entry .Chroma   a colour as 24-bit RGB packed into an `Int`, the common currency of `Chromatic`
    `Chroma` is the opaque type `anticipation.internal2.Chroma`, exported here: an `Int`
    whose low 24 bits hold red, green and blue, eight bits each, most significant first. It
    is the type every `Chromatic` instance converts to, and is itself `Chromatic`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val orange: Chroma = Chroma(255, 128, 0)
    orange.red        // 255
    orange.underlying // 0xff8000
    ```

    The `red`, `green` and `blue` extensions mask the component, so the top eight bits of
    the `Int` are never part of the colour; `underlying` gives the packed `Int`.

entry .internal2   the home of the opaque `Chroma` type
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: `Chroma` is exported from here to the `anticipation` package, and
      its page there has the full account.
  entry .Chroma   a packed 24-bit RGB colour; see `anticipation.Chroma`
    detail
        The opaque type `Chroma <: Matchable`, an `Int` at runtime.
    entry .apply   a `Chroma` from a packed `Int`, or from red, green and blue components
        Each component is masked to eight bits.
    entry .chromatic   a `Chroma` is `Chromatic` as itself
    entry .underlying   erased evidence that a `Chroma` is represented by an `Int`
  entry .underlying   the packed `Int` of a `Chroma`
  entry .red     the red component of a `Chroma`, 0 to 255
  entry .green   the green component of a `Chroma`, 0 to 255
  entry .blue    the blue component of a `Chroma`, 0 to 255

entry .Channel   a phantom channel of a packed pixel layout, such as `Red[10]`, with match types over layouts
  detail
      A channel is a colour component with a bit depth, never instantiated: `Red[10]` is ten
      bits of red. A pixel **layout** is a tuple of channels, most significant first, such as
      `(Red[10], Green[12], Blue[10])`, and iridescence's pixel types are parameterised by
      one. Each channel family carries a literal label (`"red"`, `"cyan"`, …) by which the
      match types in the companion find it within a layout; the hierarchy is open, so a
      library may add channels with labels of its own.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      type Rgb565 = (Channel.Red[5], Channel.Green[6], Channel.Blue[5])
      summon[anticipation.Channel.TotalBits[Rgb565] =:= 16]
      summon[anticipation.Channel.Storage[Rgb565] =:= Short]
      ```

      `Channel` is not exported to the `soundness` umbrella, where the name belongs to a
      WebSocket channel; reach it as `anticipation.Channel`. The channel families `Red`,
      `Green`, `Blue` and the rest are exported.
  refer anticipation.Channel.Red
  entry .Label   the literal label of a channel: `Label[Red[8]]` is `"red"`
  entry .Bits   the bit depth of a channel: `Bits[Red[8]]` is `8`
  entry .TotalBits   the total bit width of a layout
  entry .Has   whether a layout has a channel with a label, as a literal `Boolean`
  entry .Shift   the bit offset of a labelled channel within a layout
      The total width of the channels packed below it. It does not reduce for a layout
      without the channel, which statically gates accessors such as `.red` or `.cyan` to
      layouts which have it.
  entry .Depth   the bit depth of a labelled channel within a layout
  entry .Storage   the narrowest primitive holding one pixel of a layout: `Byte`, `Short`, `Int` or `Long`

  entry .Red   a pixel channel of red, of a given bit depth, labelled `"red"`
  entry .Green   a pixel channel of green, of a given bit depth, labelled `"green"`
  entry .Blue   a pixel channel of blue, of a given bit depth, labelled `"blue"`
  entry .Alpha   a pixel channel of opacity, of a given bit depth, labelled `"alpha"`
  entry .Cyan   a pixel channel of cyan, for CMYK layouts, labelled `"cyan"`
  entry .Magenta   a pixel channel of magenta, for CMYK layouts, labelled `"magenta"`
  entry .Yellow   a pixel channel of yellow, for CMYK layouts, labelled `"yellow"`
  entry .Key   a pixel channel of black (the K of CMYK), labelled `"key"`
  entry .Grey   a pixel channel of grey intensity, for monochrome layouts, labelled `"grey"`

entry .Graphical   a typeclass reading any image as a grid of `Chroma` pixels
  detail
      `image is Graphical` exposes an image's dimensions and its pixels as packed RGB, so a
      consumer can draw an image of any library's type: escapade renders any `Graphical`
      value on a terminal, and hallucination's `Raster` provides an instance.
  refer anticipation.Chroma
  entry #width    the image's width in pixels
  entry #height   the image's height in pixels
  entry #pixel    the colour of the pixel at a column and row

entry .Focusable   a typeclass locating an element in a document, in a browser driver's terms
  detail
      `value is Focusable` names a WebDriver locating strategy (`#strategy`: `css selector`,
      `xpath`, `link text`, `tag name`) and renders a value in it (`#focus`), so tarantula's
      `element` and `\` accept a selector, an XPath, an HTML tag or plain link text without
      depending on the libraries defining them. Those libraries (cataclysm, xylophone,
      honeycomb, nomenclature) provide instances in their own companions; the companion here
      makes `Text` focus as link text.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class TestId(name: Text)
      given TestId is Focusable = Focusable(t"css selector", id => t"[data-testid='${id.name}']")
      ```

      It is a pure typeclass: the rendering function must capture nothing.
  entry .apply   a `Focusable` from a strategy name and a pure rendering function
  entry .text   `Text` focuses as link text: the element whose link has exactly that text
  entry #strategy   the WebDriver locating strategy, such as `css selector` or `xpath`
  entry #focus   the value rendered in the locating strategy's syntax
